From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: amdv: hold a dual-layer BL frame until its EL is queued

amdv_wait_metadata_v1() will not let a profile-7 dual-layer base-layer frame be
toggled until its enhancement layer is sitting in the dvel receiver queue. That
is the whole point of the function's is_dovi_dual_layer_frame() block, and it is
what keeps the two layers in step.

amdv_wait_metadata_v2() has no such block. It is the live path on S6 - the
dispatcher picks it whenever multi_dv_mode is set, which register_dv_stb2.6_
functions does - so on this hardware nothing has ever paired the layers. A BL
whose EL has not arrived yet is toggled on its own; the EL that follows is then
taken by the NEXT BL in amdv_parse_metadata_v2_stb(), whose dvel_vf_get()
accepts whatever is at the head of the queue.

That offset is permanent. The tree's only realignment mechanism -
recycle_dvel_vf_put(), driven by the difference between the two decoders'
vf_put_count - lives in the video_render receiver, and a Blu-ray FEL stream is
forced to STREAM_TYPE_STREAM, whose vfm chain terminates in amvideo instead.
video.c has no equivalent. So a single unpaired BL leaves every later composite
built from a mismatched enhancement layer for the rest of the title.

So port the wait, bounded. A wait that cannot end is worse than the bug it
fixes: dolby_vision_need_wait() returning 1 makes the vsync handler skip the
toggle, so an unbounded wait is a frozen video plane - precisely what a BD-J
still (EL decoder idle, no further EL) or a dead EL decoder would produce. The
bound is not just insurance either: BL and EL are master/slave on one HEVC core
sharing an input stream, so a long BL hold can itself back-pressure EL
production.

  - amdv_el_wait_max vsyncs, default 2 (module param, 0 disables the gate).
    On expiry the BL goes through and patch 0012 composites it BL-only, so the
    failure mode of this patch is the behaviour that shipped before it. Two
    rather than more because a held vsync is not free: video.c toggles the late
    frame next vsync and, if the following one has already expired, toggles it
    in the same vsync and counts a drop. An EL more than two vsyncs behind its
    BL is a broken pipeline, not a race worth waiting out.

  - a give-up latch per instance, so a title whose EL never arrives pays the
    budget once instead of on every frame. It self-heals: the next successful
    peek clears it. Its log line is ratelimited, because that same self-heal
    means an EL queue flickering empty would otherwise emit one line per frame
    and pr_dv_error is a bare pr_info.

The predicate is vf_is_fel() rather than is_dovi_dual_layer_frame(), for two
reasons. It is a plain read of vf->src_fmt where the latter calls into the
decoder, and this runs in the vsync hard IRQ on every frame - v2 previously made
no decoder call at all once core1 was on, and it should stay that way. And it
excludes MEL per frame via parse_ret_flags, which the obvious
dv_inst[].last_mel_mode cannot: that field is written in
amdv_parse_metadata_v2_stb(), i.e. after this runs, so it is stale on exactly
the first frame of a title and every dual-stream MEL disc would pay the full
budget before latching. A MEL enhancement layer is metadata only and is bypassed
to the provider with VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL, so it never reaches
the dvel queue; waiting on one could only ever time out.

Gated to dv_id 0 because dvel_vf_peek() reads the single global EL receiver
queue, which belongs to the main pipeline. The layer test is "not VD2" rather
than "is VD1" deliberately: dv_inst[].layer_id is assigned in
amdolby_vision_process(), after this runs, so it is still VD_PATH_MAX on the
first frames of a title - the very ones the gate exists for. It only has to
reject a PIP frame, which do_pipx_toggle_frame() can route here because
dolby_vision_need_wait() passes VD1_PATH regardless of the path it peeked.

This is deliberately not a port of v1's pts matching, and the log wording says
so. v1 walks the queue discarding older ELs, but every comparison it makes is
gated on FLAG_CHECK_ES_PTS, which dolby_vision_flags does not carry by default
(FLAG_BYPASS_VPP | FLAG_FORCE_CVM) - so in this configuration v1's loop already
accepts the first EL it finds regardless of pts, exactly like the parse path's
dvel_vf_get(). The condition being fixed is "the EL queue is empty", so that is
what the gate tests.

The counters are cleared in dv_vf_light_reg_provider()/unreg_provider() beside
amdv_el_disable, which covers provider teardown on title change. A seek that
does not tear the provider down (PROVIDER_RESET) carries the latch across; that
direction is safe - the gate stays off - so it is left alone.

Signed-off-by: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
---
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.h
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.h
@@ -596,6 +596,9 @@
 	/* enhanced layer */
 	bool el_flag;
 	bool el_halfsize_flag;
+	/* BL/EL pairing gate, see amdv_wait_metadata_v2() */
+	unsigned int el_wait_count;
+	bool el_wait_giveup;
 	/* frame width & height */
 	u32 video_width;
 	u32 video_height;
--- a/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
+++ b/drivers/media/enhancement/amdolby_vision/amdv.c
@@ -206,6 +206,23 @@
 module_param(amdv_wait_delay, uint, 0664);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(amdv_wait_delay, "\n amdv_wait_delay\n");
 
+/* Vsyncs a dual-layer BL frame may be held waiting for its EL to arrive.
+ * amdv_wait_metadata_v1 waits unbounded; on the v2 path an unbounded wait
+ * would freeze the video plane outright whenever the EL stops coming (a
+ * BD-J still, a dead EL decoder), so the wait is bounded and the BL is let
+ * through on expiry - patch 0012 then composites it BL-only. 0 disables the
+ * gate entirely and restores the old always-let-it-through behaviour.
+ *
+ * Two, not more: a held vsync is not free. video.c toggles the late frame on
+ * the next vsync and, if the one after is already expired, toggles that in the
+ * same vsync and counts a drop (video_drop_vf_cnt). timestamp_vpts stalls for
+ * the hold too. An EL more than two vsyncs behind its BL is a broken pipeline,
+ * not a race worth waiting out.
+ */
+static uint amdv_el_wait_max = 2;
+module_param(amdv_el_wait_max, uint, 0664);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(amdv_el_wait_max, "\n amdv_el_wait_max\n");
+
 /* reset 1st fake frame (bit 0)*/
 /*   and other fake frames (bit 1)*/
 /*   and other toggle frames (bit 2) */
@@ -2241,6 +2258,14 @@
 		crc_count = 0;
 		crc_bypass_count = 0;
 		amdv_el_disable = 0;
+		/* the EL queue is torn down with the provider - a wait budget
+		 * or a give-up carried into the next title would be about a
+		 * stream that no longer exists
+		 */
+		for (i = 0; i < NUM_INST; i++) {
+			dv_inst[i].el_wait_count = 0;
+			dv_inst[i].el_wait_giveup = false;
+		}
 	}
 	vfm_path_on = false;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&amdv_lock, flags);
@@ -2281,6 +2306,14 @@
 		crc_count = 0;
 		crc_bypass_count = 0;
 		amdv_el_disable = 0;
+		/* the EL queue is torn down with the provider - a wait budget
+		 * or a give-up carried into the next title would be about a
+		 * stream that no longer exists
+		 */
+		for (i = 0; i < NUM_INST; i++) {
+			dv_inst[i].el_wait_count = 0;
+			dv_inst[i].el_wait_giveup = false;
+		}
 	}
 	vfm_path_on = true;
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&amdv_lock, flags);
@@ -11808,6 +11841,79 @@
 		}
 	}
 
+	/* Hold a dual-layer BL frame until its EL is queued.
+	 *
+	 * amdv_wait_metadata_v1 does this (its is_dovi_dual_layer_frame block)
+	 * and v2 never did, so on the v2 path - the live path on S6 - a BL
+	 * whose EL has not landed yet is toggled on its own. The EL then pairs
+	 * with the NEXT BL in amdv_parse_metadata_v2_stb(), whose dvel_vf_get()
+	 * takes the queue head unconditionally, and the stream stays one frame
+	 * out of phase from then on. Nothing corrects it: the only realignment
+	 * in the tree (recycle_dvel_vf_put(), driven by the two decoders'
+	 * vf_put_count) lives in the video_render receiver, and this chain
+	 * terminates in amvideo instead - see video.c, which has no equivalent.
+	 * So a single miss is permanent for the rest of the title, which is why
+	 * a gate this small is worth having.
+	 *
+	 * The predicate is vf_is_fel() rather than is_dovi_dual_layer_frame():
+	 *  - it is a plain read of vf->src_fmt, where the latter calls into the
+	 *    decoder. This runs in the vsync hard IRQ on every frame, and v2
+	 *    previously made no such call at all once core1 was on.
+	 *  - it excludes MEL per frame (parse_ret_flags != 1). The obvious
+	 *    dv_inst[].last_mel_mode cannot: it is written in
+	 *    amdv_parse_metadata_v2_stb(), which runs AFTER this, so it is
+	 *    stale on exactly the first frame of a title - and every
+	 *    dual-stream MEL disc would pay the full budget before latching.
+	 * A MEL enhancement layer is metadata only and is bypassed to the
+	 * provider, so it never reaches the dvel queue; waiting on one could
+	 * only ever time out.
+	 *
+	 * Bounds, because a wait that cannot end is worse than the bug: at most
+	 * amdv_el_wait_max vsyncs, then the BL goes through and patch 0012
+	 * composites it BL-only - so an expiry degrades to the behaviour that
+	 * shipped before this patch, not to black. The give-up latch then stops
+	 * further waiting until a pairing succeeds, so a title whose EL never
+	 * arrives pays the budget once rather than on every frame. The bound is
+	 * doing real work, not just insurance: BL and EL are master/slave on one
+	 * HEVC core sharing an input stream, so a long BL hold can itself
+	 * back-pressure EL production.
+	 *
+	 * dv_id 0 only - dvel_vf_peek() reads the single global EL receiver
+	 * queue, which belongs to the main pipeline. The layer test is written
+	 * as "not VD2" rather than "is VD1" on purpose: layer_id is assigned in
+	 * amdolby_vision_process(), i.e. after this runs, so it is still
+	 * VD_PATH_MAX on the first frames of a title - the very ones this gate
+	 * exists for. It only has to reject a PIP frame, which do_pipx_toggle_
+	 * frame() can route here because dolby_vision_need_wait() passes
+	 * VD1_PATH regardless of the path it peeked.
+	 */
+	if (amdv_el_wait_max && vd_path == VD1_PATH && dv_id == 0 &&
+	    dv_inst[dv_id].layer_id != VD2_PATH &&
+	    vf_is_fel(vf)) {
+		if (dvel_vf_peek()) {
+			dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_count = 0;
+			dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_giveup = false;
+		} else if (!dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_giveup) {
+			if (dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_count < amdv_el_wait_max) {
+				dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_count++;
+				if (debug_dolby & 2)
+					pr_dv_dbg("=== bl(%p-%lld) wait el %u/%u ===\n",
+						  vf, vf->pts_us64,
+						  dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_count,
+						  amdv_el_wait_max);
+				return 1;
+			}
+			dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_giveup = true;
+			dv_inst[dv_id].el_wait_count = 0;
+			/* ratelimited: the latch clears on any successful peek,
+			 * so an EL queue flickering empty gives one of these per
+			 * affected frame - and pr_dv_error is a bare pr_info
+			 */
+			pr_info_ratelimited("AMDV ERROR: bl(%p-%lld) el queue still empty after %u vsyncs, compositing bl-only\n",
+					    vf, vf->pts_us64, amdv_el_wait_max);
+		}
+	}
+
 	if (!dv_inst[dv_id].amdv_wait_init && !dv_core1[layer_id].core1_on) {
 		ret = is_amdv_frame(vf);
 		if (ret) {
